        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "",
          "# Demo 2c: Does the Model Travel? Spatial Leakage with the SLX Classifier (OML4Py)",
          "",
          "**HrOUG 2026 · Spatial Meets AI** · Which ZET stops need shade first?",
          "",
          "Surface temperature is measured where Landsat sees the ground on a clear day. To score places without a measurement (a new district, a planned square, another city), a model has to predict heat from what is known everywhere: land cover. The SLX classifier is a logistic regression that adds the **spatial lag** of the features, the average land cover of the neighbouring cells, so each cell is classified with its neighbourhood.",
          "",
          "The catch is **spatial leakage**. Neighbouring cells look alike. With a random train/test split, every test cell has neighbours in the training data, and the score looks better than it will be in a new area. Leakage hurts most when the features borrow from neighbours, as the spatial lag does. Holding out a whole area shows the honest score.",
          "",
          "This notebook answers three questions:",
          "1. **How good does the model look the usual way?** SLX against a plain logistic regression, scored on a random 80/20 split.",
          "2. **How good is it really?** The same models trained without Novi Zagreb and scored on Novi Zagreb, the two city districts south of the Sava.",
          "3. **Where does it go wrong?** The held-out predictions on the map of Novi Zagreb.",
          "",
          "Adapted from the Oracle sample notebook *OML4Py Spatial AI SLX Classifier* (Copyright (c) 2025 Oracle Corporation, [The Universal Permissive License (UPL), Version 1.0](https://oss.oracle.com/licenses/upl/))."
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "html",
        "visualizationConfig" : null,
        "hideCode" : true,
        "hideResult" : false,
        "hideGutter" : true,
        "hideVizConfig" : false,
        "hideInIFrame" : false,
        "enabled" : true,
