        "result" : {
          "interpreter" : "md.low",
          "taskStatus" : "SUCCESS",
          "status" : "SUCCESS",
          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<h1 id=\"demo-2c-does-the-model-travel-spatial-leakage-with-the-slx-classifier-oml4py\">Demo 2c: Does the Model Travel? Spatial Leakage with the SLX Classifier (OML4Py)</h1>\n<p><strong>HrOUG 2026 · Spatial Meets AI</strong> · Which ZET stops need shade first?</p>\n<p>Surface temperature is measured where Landsat sees the ground on a clear day. To score places without a measurement (a new district, a planned square, another city), a model has to predict heat from what is known everywhere: land cover. The SLX classifier is a logistic regression that adds the <strong>spatial lag</strong> of the features, the average land cover of the neighbouring cells, so each cell is classified with its neighbourhood.</p>\n<p>The catch is <strong>spatial leakage</strong>. Neighbouring cells look alike. With a random train/test split, every test cell has neighbours in the training data, and the score looks better than it will be in a new area. Leakage hurts most when the features borrow from neighbours, as the spatial lag does. Holding out a whole area shows the honest score.</p>\n<p>This notebook answers three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>How good does the model look the usual way?</strong> SLX against a plain logistic regression, scored on a random 80/20 split.</li>\n<li><strong>How good is it really?</strong> The same models trained without Novi Zagreb and scored on Novi Zagreb, the two city districts south of the Sava.</li>\n<li><strong>Where does it go wrong?</strong> The held-out predictions on the map of Novi Zagreb.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Adapted from the Oracle sample notebook <em>OML4Py Spatial AI SLX Classifier</em> (Copyright (c) 2025 Oracle Corporation, <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://oss.oracle.com/licenses/upl/\">The Universal Permissive License (UPL), Version 1.0</a>).</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
